WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.472 --> 00:00:03.139
(clock ticking)

00:00:04.520 --> 00:00:08.310
- Let me take you back to July 16th, 1945.

00:00:08.310 --> 00:00:10.470
On this day, a team of nuclear engineers

00:00:10.470 --> 00:00:14.243
detonated the world's
first nuclear device ever,

00:00:15.340 --> 00:00:17.320
right here in New Mexico.

00:00:17.320 --> 00:00:19.620
It's hard to overstate this moment.

00:00:19.620 --> 00:00:22.823
This moment ended an era
and started a new one.

00:00:27.541 --> 00:00:31.000
(bomb explodes)

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:35.100
21 days later, a U.S.
bomber flew over a city

00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:38.360
in Southern Japan with one
of these bombs in its belly

00:00:38.360 --> 00:00:41.770
and it dropped it from
30,000 feet above the ground.

00:00:41.770 --> 00:00:43.980
The bomb dropped for 43 seconds

00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:47.310
before exploding a few hundred
meters above the ground,

00:00:47.310 --> 00:00:50.143
turning this city into dust.

00:00:51.420 --> 00:00:53.890
This was the first time
that a nuclear weapon

00:00:53.890 --> 00:00:56.630
had ever been used to kill humans.

00:00:56.630 --> 00:00:58.930
And three days later, the
U.S. dropped another one

00:00:58.930 --> 00:01:00.380
on another city in Japan.

00:01:00.380 --> 00:01:04.030
360,000 people, most of them civilians,

00:01:04.030 --> 00:01:05.470
dead from these attacks.

00:01:05.470 --> 00:01:07.040
The president at the time, Harry Truman,

00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:09.690
was on a ship crossing
the Atlantic from Europe,

00:01:09.690 --> 00:01:12.070
his voice broadcasting across the world.

00:01:12.070 --> 00:01:16.730
- An American airplane
dropped one bomb on Hiroshima.

00:01:16.730 --> 00:01:21.553
That bomb has more power
than 20,000 tons of TNT.

00:01:24.090 --> 00:01:27.670
- The nuclear era had begun,
and soon the Soviet Union

00:01:27.670 --> 00:01:29.690
was developing a bomb of their own.

00:01:29.690 --> 00:01:30.800
It became a race,

00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:32.940
who could develop the
biggest and best bomb.

00:01:32.940 --> 00:01:36.380
But this nuclear bomb
technology was unpredictable

00:01:36.380 --> 00:01:38.300
and not really well understood.

00:01:38.300 --> 00:01:40.730
The only way to keep
developing it was to test it,

00:01:40.730 --> 00:01:43.470
so that they could understand
how to build these things.

00:01:43.470 --> 00:01:45.650
The first test was here in the U.S.,

00:01:45.650 --> 00:01:48.930
but soon the tests moved
far away from the U.S.

00:01:48.930 --> 00:01:50.093
deep into the Pacific.

00:01:52.010 --> 00:01:55.260
This is the story of a bomb,
but it's also the story

00:01:55.260 --> 00:01:57.300
of the cultural shock waves

00:01:57.300 --> 00:02:00.210
that these far away tests
created here in the U.S.

00:02:00.210 --> 00:02:01.080
and around the world.

00:02:01.080 --> 00:02:03.750
From French swimsuits to propaganda films

00:02:03.750 --> 00:02:05.430
meant to deceive the nation,

00:02:05.430 --> 00:02:08.450
to monsters and Japanese protest films,

00:02:08.450 --> 00:02:10.710
this story has some twists and turns.

00:02:10.710 --> 00:02:13.340
So buckle up, and let
me tell you the story

00:02:13.340 --> 00:02:17.043
of why the U.S. bombed the
(beep) out of these islands.

00:02:18.130 --> 00:02:20.890
- A bomb will not start a
chain reaction in the water.

00:02:20.890 --> 00:02:23.490
Converting it all to gas,
letting all the ships

00:02:23.490 --> 00:02:26.090
on all the oceans drop down in the bottom.

00:02:26.090 --> 00:02:28.970
- [Man] We have spent more than $2 billion

00:02:28.970 --> 00:02:32.550
on the greatest scientific
gamble in history.

00:02:32.550 --> 00:02:33.853
And we have won.

00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:39.713
- One second.

